## The correct setup for DLBC's personal Gmail accounts

Your pastors must each connect their own Google account to DLBC once. The backend will then create each consultation's Calendar event under the assigned pastor's primary calendar, using that pastor's OAuth refresh token.

This solves the organizer problem without a service account or Workspace domain-wide delegation.

There is one important limitation: Google says that for a meeting organized by a personal Google Account, only the meeting creator can admit uninvited participants. It also says the full Restricted meeting-access option is not available to consumer accounts.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://support.google.com\&sz=32)

Google Meet Help



I will therefore configure the member as an uninvited guest: the member gets the saved link from DLBC, but is not added as a Calendar attendee. That makes the member request admission rather than receiving automatic access as an invitee. The pastor, as the actual meeting creator, is the person who can admit them.

What this implementation can guarantee: the pastor owns the event and is its creator; both users retrieve the same Firestore URL; the member is not invited through Calendar.

What Calendar-only cannot guarantee: programmatically forcing every personal Gmail meeting into Restricted mode. You must test the pastor's Meet access behavior with a second account before relying on it for confidential consultations. The Calendar API supports creating the conference, but does not expose the Meet host access settings in the event-insert request.&#x20;

